  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-\\sqrt{3},1).$ Find the polar coordinates of $P.$ Give the polar angle in radians and do not round the answer.\n\nThe polar coordinates of $P$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(\\frac{1}{-\\sqrt{3}})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the second quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi $ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(2,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$",
